[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ADDSS`/`SUBSS`/`MULSS`/`DIVSS` return the **destination** operand's NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (quieted), and gcc `-O0` and LLVM choose different destination registers for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the same C expression. Confirmed by disassembling both `.so`s. Fixed by adding
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m explicit `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul`/`fdiv` helpers in `src/lib.rs` that encode the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m SSE NaN-precedence rule in software, with gcc's destination operand passed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first — making the result codegen-independent instead of relying on how
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `a op b` happens to be register-allocated. For every non-NaN input these are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit-identical to the plain operators.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
