[38;5;252m[1m## What the library is[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src/src/lib.c[0m is a slice of cute_c2 (2D collision routines) — 30 functions, 28 with external linkage plus 2 [38;5;10mstatic inline[0m — with a fixed-input [38;5;10mpoly_ray[0m driver declared in [38;5;10minclude/lib.h[0m. No features, no binary target on either side.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Divergences found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Five, all in [38;5;10mtranslation/src/lib.rs[0m, all the same root cause. x86 [38;5;10mADDSS[0m/[38;5;10mSUBSS[0m/[38;5;10mMULSS[0m/[38;5;10mDIVSS[0m are two-operand instructions that return the [1mdestination[22m operand's NaN payload (quieted) when both operands are NaN, and gcc [38;5;10m-O0[0m and LLVM pick different destination registers for the same C expression. Concretely, C's [38;5;10ma.x * b.x + a.y * b.y[0m compiles to an [38;5;10maddss[0m whose destination is the [3msecond[23m product, while Rust's compiles to one whose destination is the [3mfirst[23m — so [38;5;10mc2Dot({-0.0, NaN}, {-inf, -3.4e38})[0m returned [38;5;10m0x7FC00000[0m from C and [38;5;10m0xFFC00000[0m from Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I confirmed the destination operand at every arithmetic site by disassembling both [38;5;10m.so[0ms, then replaced source-order matching (brittle — it depends on register allocation) with explicit [38;5;10mfadd[0m/[38;5;10mfsub[0m/[38;5;10mfmul[0m/[38;5;10mfdiv[0m helpers that encode the SSE NaN-precedence rule in software. For non-NaN inputs they are bit-identical to the plain operators. Affected functions: [38;5;10mc2Dot[0m, [38;5;10mc2Add[0m (gcc's [38;5;10maddss[0m destination is the [3mright[23m operand), [38;5;10mc2MulmvT[0m, [38;5;10mc2Mulrv[0m, [38;5;10mc2MulrvT[0m, [38;5;10mc2RaytoAABB[0m ([38;5;10mout->t = t_i * A.t[0m — destination is [38;5;10mA.t[0m), and [38;5;10mc2RaytoCapsule[0m (two sites).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
